1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,040
Listen to a short talk on biology.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:06,058
Today, we are going to continue our discussion of social insects, focusing on the

3
00:00:06,058 --> 00:00:06,962
Argentine ant,

4
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:11,120
which as you might guess is a species of ant that are natives to Argentina.

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:15,726
We’ll consider what happened to this type of ant after some members of the

6
00:00:15,726 --> 00:00:18,180
species move to California from their original habitat.

7
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:24,230
Ok, well, in Argentina, these Argentine ants behave like most ant species around the world.

8
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:28,244
They fight other ants of the same species if those ants are from some

9
00:00:28,244 --> 00:00:28,725
other nests.

10
00:00:28,725 --> 00:00:33,020
But the Argentine ants living in California behave differently.

11
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:36,453
Ants from different nests form a single large colony.

12
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:41,030
Within this colony, there is little aggression among ants from different nests.

13
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:45,752
And when they fight insects from outside their colony, the Argentine ants can quickly

14
00:00:45,752 --> 00:00:48,787
recruit a huge army from their network of nests.

15
00:00:48,787 --> 00:00:52,160
This of course gives them advantages over other ants’ species.

16
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:57,270
So then, why do Argentine ants behave differently in California than they do in Argentina?

17
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:04,280
Well, using genetic testing, researches found that all the Argentine ants in California were very similar genetically.

18
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:04,976
You see,

19
00:01:04,976 --> 00:01:10,196
when the first Argentine ants came to California, their population must have been very small，

20
00:01:10,196 --> 00:01:15,068
and all the later generations of Argentine ants there must be descended from the

21
00:01:15,068 --> 00:01:16,112
same few ancestors.

22
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:18,200
So they are all closely related.

23
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:19,800
This discovery is important,

24
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,400
because for most social insects, membership in a colony is based on how closely

25
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,000
related they are genetically.
